<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nobles of the Sword</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as noblesse d'épée, noblesse de race, noblesse ancienne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nobles of the Sword were the oldest class of the French nobility, dating from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knighthood">knightly</a> class, they owed military service, usually to the king, in return for feudal landed estates, and took their name from their right to bear a sword. In later centuries a family had to hold the status for at least three generations. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> onwards kings created a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobles-of-the-robe">nobility of the robe</a> through judicial and administrative offices, to the old nobility's anger. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> the nobles of the robe largely replaced them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>; the nobles of the sword kept greater prestige, tax exemption and much of the officer class, but usually had lower incomes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nobles-of-the-sword.jpg" alt="Nobles of the Sword" width="330" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Gabriel_de_Rochechouart,_Duke_of_Mortemart_wearing_the_Order_of_the_Holy_Spirit_(Versailles,_unknown_artist).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knighthood">knightly</a> service of the nobles of the sword was owed usually to the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but in some cases to other monarchs, such as the Plantagenet kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The term is largely synonymous with noblesse de race, nobility of family, and noblesse ancienne, old nobility. It set them apart from the nobility of chancery, who held high offices under the king; the nobility of letters, ennobled by royal letters patent; the nobility of the bell, mayors and merchants' provosts of certain towns; and the military nobility, officers of the army or navy not born into the old nobility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Their standing rested on the military service they owed the king for their landholdings, which passed from father to son, but they also held posts in every branch of government, in the provinces, in national government and at court, and were given control of the French provinces. In the Estates General they sat with the Second Estate, where they traditionally had more power than the nobles of the robe. In the 17th century the nobles of the robe had no right to bear a sword, which kept the line between the two groups clear, and lower-ranking noblemen could raise their standing through military service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 17th century the old nobility demanded that the nobles of the robe be limited in their access to the court, but the government, seeking income, went on selling offices. The sales also served the Crown by weakening the old nobility and making it less able to revolt, though the kings still needed good relations with the class that officered their army and navy. Nobles of the robe, paid salaries by the king, voted for his policy, while the more independent nobles of the sword did not.</p><p>The quarrel weakened the balance of power before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. By the historian Gordon Wright's figure, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had 80,000 nobles in 1789. The attempts of the nobility of the sword to keep its old monopoly of power drew the new nobility's interests towards the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>, creating a powerful force for change in French society in the late 18th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nobles of the Sword” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
